---
import PageLayout from '@/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import QueryBuilderDemo from '@/components/query-builder/QueryBuilderDemo';
import FigmaFlow, { SyntaxSequence } from '@/components/query-builder/FigmaFlow';
import '@/components/query-builder/query-builder.css';
---

<PageLayout title="Query Builder — Patrick Morgan" description="Making a powerful graph query language approachable. An interactive presentation of my work at JupiterOne." image="/images/projects/query/thumbnail-query-light-wide.webp" noindex>
  <article class="qb-page">
    <nav class="qb-project-nav" aria-label="Project navigation"><a href="/work">← Work</a><span><img src="/images/logos/square-jupiterone.svg" width="22" height="22" alt="" />JupiterOne</span><a href="#outcome">The outcome ↓</a></nav>
    <header class="qb-intro">
      <h1>Powerful queries.<br /><span>A gentler learning curve.</span></h1>
      <p>I designed a visual query builder that helped people explore their security data—without first learning a language.</p>
      <div class="qb-project-facts"><span>Lead Product Designer</span><span>Designer + engineer</span><span>JupiterOne · 2022–2023</span></div>
    </header>

    <section class="qb-hero-stage" aria-label="Query builder walkthrough"><QueryBuilderDemo client:load /></section>

    <section class="qb-story-block">
      <div class="qb-story-copy"><h2>A blank field.<br />A world of possibilities.<br /><span>No obvious way in.</span></h2><p>JupiterOne connected security data across hundreds of classes and thousands of types. But new customers had to know what to ask—and how to ask it in J1QL.</p><p>The language was powerful. Getting started was the hard part.</p></div>
      <SyntaxSequence client:load />
    </section>

    <section class="qb-model-section" aria-labelledby="model-title">
      <div class="qb-section-intro"><h2 id="model-title">First, make the possibilities visible.</h2><p>A browsable model turns an unfamiliar environment into recognizable things. Categories give you a way in. Classes and types give you precision.</p></div>
      <FigmaFlow sequence="select" client:load />
    </section>

    <section class="qb-craft-section" aria-labelledby="craft-title">
      <div class="qb-section-intro"><h2 id="craft-title">Teach the language<br /><span>through the interface.</span></h2><p>Keep the structure of J1QL. Give people familiar controls to work with it. Every interaction builds confidence as well as a query.</p></div>
      <div class="qb-detail-grid">
        <div><FigmaFlow sequence="filter" client:load /><div class="qb-detail-copy"><h3>Refine the question.</h3><p>A property, an operator, and a value. Familiar choices give the language a tangible structure.</p></div></div>
        <div><FigmaFlow sequence="direction" client:load /><div class="qb-detail-copy"><h3>Make relationships explicit.</h3><p>A small direction control makes a subtle graph concept visible.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="qb-approach"><div><h2>Two people.<br /><span>One shared problem.</span></h2></div><div><p>I partnered with an engineer to work through the data model, customer needs, and the realities of a stateful interface. Our security and Solutions Architecture teams helped ground the work.</p><p>We prototyped in code where complexity demanded it. I used Figma to explore and communicate the interactions.</p><p class="qb-future-note">The first release laid the groundwork for queries to live as both code and UI. Full parity was the longer-term direction. The walkthrough above shows that design intent.</p></div></section>

    <section id="outcome" class="qb-outcome" aria-labelledby="outcome-title"><h2 id="outcome-title">A much shorter path<br />to the first useful query.</h2><div class="qb-outcome-numbers"><div><span>21</span><p>days before</p></div><span class="qb-outcome-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div><span>1</span><p>day after</p></div></div><p>Average time after data integration to writing and saving a first custom J1QL query, as reported in the original project presentation.</p></section>

    <footer class="qb-project-footer"><div><h2>The power was already there.<br /><span>The interface opened it up.</span></h2><p>Interaction design · Systems thinking · Prototyping</p></div><div class="qb-resource-links"><a href="https://www.figma.com/deck/UlMqhnukdHWCr2yrdVgcx2/Slides---J1-Query-Builder?node-id=12-921" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original presentation ↗</a><a href="https://youtu.be/74FdNELZCKU" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Watch the walkthrough ↗</a><a href="/work">Back to Work →</a></div></footer>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
